# tests/modifiers/malformed_and_gate.rxt — module `modifiers` (MOD-0.5c):
# two things that are NOT the module's semantic producers but ARE its
# recognition surface — [MOD-0.5a] ruling 6 (malformed option-setting runs
# are the module's to diagnose) and ruling 2 (per-letter refusals `m` ->
# 'assertions', `J` -> 'named-groups') — plus a small set of GATE-OFF pins
# (today's real, already-correct behaviour, no producer required).
#
# ---- malformed vs non-construct ------------------------------------------
# `(?i-m-s)` IS a recognised option-setting run, just a MALFORMED one (a
# hyphen appearing twice — PCRE2 error 194 "invalid hyphen in option
# setting", measured against libpcre2 10.46). `(?iZ)` is NOT a construct at
# all: `Z` is never a recognised modifier letter, so it never even reaches
# the module's malformed-run diagnosis — it is base-grammar's "unrecognized
# character after (? or (?-", independent of whether `modifiers` is
# enabled, TODAY and permanently (D26: the WORDING need not match PCRE2's
# error 111 for the same input; the shape does — see the same probe run).
#
# ---- per-letter refusal, WHY belongs to tests/reject/ ---------------------
# `m` and `J` are recognised modifier letters whose SEMANTIC home is a
# different module (ruling 2). With ONLY `features modifiers` enabled they
# must still refuse — but a `.rxt` `perr` block asserts only THAT a pattern
# is rejected, never WHY (tests/reject/CLAUDE.md), and the module NAME in
# the diagnostic is the whole point of ruling 2. **The WHY check (that the
# diagnostic names 'assertions'/'named-groups', not 'modifiers') needs a
# tests/reject/ entry; this file only pins THAT these stay rejected.**
# Flagged for the merge: adding those two tests/reject/ rows is part of
# [MOD-0.5c]'s landing bar, not something this corpus can discharge.
# These two blocks use a LEADING literal (`a(?m)...`, `a(?J)...`) rather
# than position 0, for the ORACLE reason below, not for any pcrec-semantic
# reason.
#
# ORACLE: every block in this file is plain, unmarked (no `# pcre2-only`)
# — NOT because python agrees these constructs are invalid PCRE (it does
# not; `(?m)^b` and `(?i-m-s)a`'s LETTERS are real PCRE), but because every
# pattern here happens to ALSO fail to compile in python re, for python's
# OWN independent reasons (a non-start-position global inline flag is a
# python SyntaxError since 3.11 — "global flags not at the start of the
# expression"; `U`/`n`/`J`/`^` are not python flag letters at all —
# "unknown extension"; `(?i-m-s)` is "missing :" since python's unscoped
# form is add-only; `Z` is "unknown flag"). `perr` only asserts THAT a
# pattern fails to compile, so this is genuine (if coincidental) two-oracle
# agreement, confirmed directly against python3 `re` 2026-08-12 for every
# pattern below — not a claim that python's REASON matches pcrec's.
#
# THE D33 §9.3 RECORD, split (this file mixes forward pins with today-
# already-correct pins — say which):
#   - the two malformed/non-construct blocks (both gate states: `(?i-m-s)a`
#     WITH features modifiers, `(?iZ)a` with and without) PASS TODAY —
#     `modifiers` being wholly unimplemented already refuses everything,
#     which is a strictly WEAKER but still-perr-satisfying reason; `(?iZ)a`
#     would perr identically before, during and after MOD-0.5 (it never
#     touches the module).
#   - the two per-letter-refusal blocks (`a(?m)^b`, `a(?J)...`) with
#     `features modifiers` ALSO pass today, and are a FORWARD PIN: they
#     will stay perr straight through MOD-0.5c's landing (m/J's real
#     modules do not exist yet) — not a §9.3 near-term flip, flagged so it
#     is not mistaken for one.
#   - the six gate-off blocks (no `features` line at all) PASS TODAY and
#     will keep passing after MOD-0.5 lands (default enabled set stays
#     empty) — these are the ones worth watching for a REGRESSION, not a
#     future flip.
# Measured 2026-08-12: all 11 blocks in this file (11 cases, all `perr`)
# already PASS against the MOD-0.5a-gate binary, 0 pattern-compile
# failures — the one file in this corpus that is fully green before the
# module lands, by design.

# ---- malformed vs non-construct, gate ON ----------------------------------

pattern (?i-m-s)a
features modifiers
perr

pattern (?iZ)a
features modifiers
perr

# ---- (?iZ) is not a construct AT ALL: stays rejected with the gate OFF too

pattern (?iZ)a
perr

# ---- per-letter refusal: m's home is 'assertions', not 'modifiers' -------

pattern a(?m)^b
features modifiers
perr

# ---- per-letter refusal: J's home is 'named-groups', not 'modifiers' -----

pattern a(?J)(?<g>x)(?<g>y)
features modifiers
perr

# ---- gate OFF: today's behaviour, no features line, must stay pinned -----

pattern a(?i)b
perr

pattern a(?U)b+
perr

pattern a(?n)(b)
perr

pattern a(?^)b
perr

pattern a(?s)b
perr

pattern a(?x)b c
perr
